senator Doug Jones is the first Democrat  elected to the Senate from Alabama in 25  years he defeated the GOP Roy Moore in a  special election last month Jones is a  member of the Homeland Security  Committee he's with us from Capitol Hill  for his first Network interview since  his swearing in earlier this month  senator Jones for we're very happy to  have you with us let me start with what  are your expectations for this speech  from the president tonight well first of  all let me thank you for having me this  morning I really appreciate it you know  I'm hoping and expecting that the  president will deliver a presidential  address not a campaign address but a  presidential address in which he lays  out not only his accomplishments this  past year but also his goals for the  future I hoping he is going to reach  across the aisle I think he's going to  be talking about unity I think that's  the best way to advance the agenda I  think my election was somewhat of a  wake-up call for America that we need to  to really get together to try to reach  across the aisle have a bipartisan  agreement on these things and get the  country moving yes your election was a  little dicey there senator Jones we're  very happy as as Norah and John said to  have you here what do you need to hear  from the president to unify the country  what exactly does he need to say well I  don't think he needs to just draw a line  in the dust and say it's my way or the  highway what I think he needs to do is  to try to lay out his goals lay out some  specifics of what he wants to do I think  there's a lot of areas both in  immigration as well as infrastructure  and some of the other things that the  president wants to move forward then I  think Democrats and Republicans alike  will embrace if the question is one  atone I think a tone is going to be very  important for him to try to reach out to  Democrats and Republicans alike  to try to make sure that we are working  together and this is not just a fight  it's just not a battle between one  faction or another but something that we  can do as Americans let me ask you then  about substance because the president is  expected to talk tonight about  immigration as well as infrastructure  and on the issue of immigration the  White House has proposed a generous  proposal in terms of a pathway to  citizenship for some almost 2 million  dreamers but at the same time wants a  lot of money 25 billion dollars for some  type of border security would you  support that  well I'm gonna look at that obviously  everyone was to increase security on the  Border's what that exactly looks like  and how much it's going to cost is going  to be the details that we really haven't  seen from the government at this point  you know it's twenty five billion  dollars is a lot of money during the  campaign the president talked about  building a wall but he said Mexico was  going to pay for well now we know it's  going to be the American public that's  going to pay for it so we have to be  careful we have to be smart with our  money but look I think that the  president has laid out goals and  objectives that need to be put on the  table and discussed I think the  Democrats are going to have that I think  other Republicans are gonna have  something I'm very optimistic about an  immigration plan going forward senator  would you give us your sense of your  party exactly on this question of  immigration you're making reasonable  sounding views here about listening to  what the president has to say there are  a lot of Senators in your party you  don't want to do a deal with this  president so can you straighten us out  here on exactly what the receptivity is  going to be to this offer from the  president sure I don't think it's a  question of doing a deal with this  president I don't think you're going to  see that at all I think they want to get  a immigration deal done obviously daca  and the dreamers are very very important  but border security is also important to  Democrats the details are going to be  something that have to be worked out we  have to look at what some people call  chain migration other people call family  reunification all of those is going to  be issues what I'm hoping we can do is  focus on the two most important pillars  of immigration right now which is the  daca issue as well as border security I  think there can be broad consensus on  those two pillars and you voted with the  Republicans for the spending deal  despite not having a deal on daca  how and why did you decide to vote  against your own party well I don't  think I've voted against my own party in  particular there were other Democrats  that voted like I did I had to do a  different equation you know I campaign a  lot on the child Health Insurance  Program chip that was an important part  of my campaign it's important to my  state where 150,000 kids were getting  pink slips for their health care that  was very important that we were funding  chip for six years  Alabama also would have been hurt a lot  by a shutdown in the government we have  a lot of federal dollars that come in so  I looked at the whole picture I was not  happy to be honest with you I don't  think  continuing resolutions are a way to fund  the government we need to get back to  some regular order when we do a budget  and have that but having said that I had  to make a decision and I felt like  keeping the government open and funding  chip was the right way to go all right  senator Jones the night of your election  you said this was a this was a  fulfillment of a lifelong dream you  still feel that sir absolutely it's just  amazing I've told folks that have walked  off that Senate floor as a Senate  staffer in 1980 with mobile boss Howell  Heflin and to come back with the Vice  President Biden and my old friend  trailing me vice president Mondale vice  president pence on the floor it was just  an amazing experience and I am honored  and humbled to be here all right a lot  of work to do Thank You senator Jones  really good to see you sir  thank you thank you any time thank you  and remember to watch CBS News coverage  of the State of the Union tonight  starting at 9:00 Eastern 8:00 central  right here on CBS John Gill and I will  report here from New York and Jeff Glor  will anchor in Washington our entire  political team will provide reporting  and analysis
 

